data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_05a2030fc037c9c2006ad35ce08c6487d19f25bd75d5745702","logprobs":[],"output_index":0,"sequence_number":229,"text":"Memory has three stages: encoding, which inputs information into the memory system; storage, which retains encoded information; and retrieval, which brings information out of memory and into awareness [1].\n\nThe lecture explains forgetting as one part of memory’s fragile nature, and says memories can also become less accurate and distorted rather than remaining records of experience [2]. It describes forgetting as occurring as experiences, thoughts, and feelings mix together, with subsequent experiences able to change earlier memories [3].\n\nCaveat: The lecture passage discusses forgetting and memory distortion broadly but does not give a complete account of the mechanisms of forgetting.\nSources:\n[1] p1: \"Encoding involves the input of information into the memory system. Storage is the retention of the encoded information. Retrieval, or getting the information out of memory and back into awareness, is the third function.\"\n[2] p5: \"it turns out there's ways in which our memories get less accurate and distorted, are not actual records of our experience\"\n[3] p8: \"the raw memory is never recoverable from the mixture of subsequent experiences, thoughts, and feelings.\""}

